    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Philip II turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from a raided frontier kingdom into the master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greece">Greece</a> with a pike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phalanx">phalanx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indus">Indus</a>, spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greek-language">Greek language</a> and cities across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>.</li><li>It split among his generals after 323 BCE, but their kingdoms created the Hellenistic world that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> later absorbed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Philip II's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phalanx">phalanx</a> carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sarissa">sarissa</a>, a pike of up to about six metres, letting several ranks present their points to the enemy at once.</p></aside>
    </section>

